-- Passage rg6-main-idea-summary-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('bb205585-475f-5ebb-90b5-07e4782823a2', 'rg6-main-idea-summary-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How Bats Hunt with Sound$q$, $q$Many bats hunt at night, when it is too dark to see insects clearly. Instead of relying only on their eyes, these bats use sound to find their way and catch food. This skill is called echolocation, and it works almost like a built-in sonar system.

A hunting bat makes a series of very high clicks or calls, often through its mouth or nose. Most of these sounds are too high for human ears to hear. The sound waves travel outward until they strike an object, such as a tree branch or a moth. Then they bounce back toward the bat as echoes.

The bat's large, sensitive ears catch the returning echoes. Its brain uses them to judge how far away the object is, how big it is and which way it is moving. An echo that returns quickly means the object is close. An echo that returns slowly means it is farther away.

As a bat closes in on an insect, it calls faster and faster. Scientists who record these calls hear them speed up into a rapid buzz just before the catch. The quicker calls give the bat fresh information many times each second, so it can follow a moth that twists and dives.

Echolocation lets bats do things that would otherwise be impossible. They can weave through thick forests and dark caves without crashing. They can catch hundreds of small insects in one night, which helps keep insect numbers in balance. A sound that people cannot even hear turns out to be one of the most useful tools in the animal world.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
