-- Passage rg6-main-idea-summary-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('bcd50f3b-0be3-5ef5-ae54-f0623ed3625f', 'rg6-main-idea-summary-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How Traffic Signals Decide$q$, $q$A traffic light might seem to change colors on a simple timer. Many signals do follow a fixed schedule, but others are smarter than they look. Modern traffic signals use sensors and planning to move cars and people safely and with less waiting.

The simplest signals run on timers. Engineers study how many vehicles use each street at different hours. Then they set how long each light stays green. A busy main road might get a long green, while a quiet side street gets a short one.

Other signals respond to traffic as it arrives. Some intersections have wire loops buried under the pavement. When a car stops over a loop, its metal changes the loop's magnetic field, and the signal's controller notices that a vehicle is waiting. Other intersections use small cameras or radar units mounted on poles. If no car is waiting on a side street, the main road can keep its green light.

Signals can also work as a team. On a long avenue, engineers can link lights together so they turn green one after another. A driver moving at the speed limit may then pass through several green lights in a row. This pattern is often called a green wave.

People on foot are part of the plan too. Push buttons at crosswalks tell the controller that someone wants to cross. Many signals give walkers a few seconds head start before cars get a green light, which makes walkers easier for drivers to see.

All of these tools share one goal. By reacting to real traffic, signals help reduce waiting, wasted fuel and crashes.$q$, 'draft')
