-- Passage rg6-story-details-inference-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('307deb04-66a0-5e03-8377-4cd6637e31ea', 'rg6-story-details-inference-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-story-details-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Magpie and the Sparrows$q$, $q$In a tall elm at the edge of a meadow lived a magpie named Brightwing, who loved anything that sparkled. Each morning she flew over the meadow and the farm road, collecting bottle caps, bits of foil, and a lost silver button. She wove each treasure into her nest until it glittered like a jewelry box.

A family of sparrows lived on the branch below. When a storm tore their nest apart one spring, the father sparrow flew up to ask if his chicks could shelter in a corner of Brightwing's nest for a single night.

Brightwing spread her wings across her treasures. "There is no room," she said. "Every corner is full."

The sparrows huddled in a knot of roots at the bottom of the tree, and the rain fell all night.

By summer, Brightwing's nest had grown so heavy with metal that the branch beneath it creaked in every breeze. One windy afternoon, it cracked. The nest tipped, and caps and buttons rained down into the tall grass.

Brightwing searched the meadow until sunset and found only the silver button. She sat with it on a low fence post, too tired to fly back to the empty fork of the elm.

The sparrows had rebuilt their nest by then, small and plain, out of grass and twigs. The father sparrow looked down at the magpie for a long moment. Then he flew to the fence post and said, "There is a corner free tonight, if you would like it."$q$, 'draft')
