-- Passage rg6-text-structure-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('4efeaafb-e49f-5a41-91ac-29dfe911418f', 'rg6-text-structure-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Why Leaves Change Color$q$, $q$Each fall, the hills near many towns turn from green to gold, orange, and red. The change looks like magic, but it is really the result of a chain of causes and effects inside each leaf.

All summer, leaves are busy making food for the tree. They use a green substance called chlorophyll to capture energy from sunlight. Because leaves contain so much chlorophyll, its green color covers up every other color in the leaf.

As fall arrives, the days grow shorter and the nights grow cooler. With less sunlight, the tree begins to slow down its food making. It stops producing new chlorophyll, and the chlorophyll already in the leaves starts to break down.

As a result, colors that were hidden all summer begin to show. Yellow and orange substances have been in the leaves the whole time. Once the green fades, these colors finally appear.

Red and purple come from a different cause. In some trees, such as certain maples, sugar gets trapped in the leaves when the weather turns cool. Sunny days and cool nights turn this sugar into red coloring. That is why a fall with bright, sunny days often has the most brilliant reds.

Eventually, a thin layer of cells forms where each leaf joins its branch. This layer seals the leaf off from the tree, and the leaf drops to the ground. The tree then rests through the winter until spring brings new green leaves.$q$, 'draft')
