-- Passage rg6-theme-summary-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1c4b0c21-cdcc-54b9-9ab5-b272f28ef127', 'rg6-theme-summary-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-theme-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Solar Oven$q$, $q$Amara had a plan before the science teacher even finished explaining the project. Each team would build a solar oven from a pizza box, foil and plastic wrap. The oven that melted a chocolate square fastest would win.

"We'll line the whole inside with foil," Amara told her team. "More foil means more heat."

Joon, who rarely spoke in class, held up a sketch. "What if we put the foil on the open flap instead?" he asked. "We could tilt it to bounce sunlight into the box."

"That sounds too complicated," Amara said, and she started taping. Joon folded his sketch and put it in his pocket.

On testing day, the teams set their ovens on the sunny blacktop. After twenty minutes, Amara checked their chocolate. It had barely softened. Across the yard, two other teams were already cheering.

Amara sat down on the curb. "I don't get it," she said. "We used more foil than anyone."

Joon hesitated. Then he took out the folded sketch. "The foil inside just keeps the light in," he said. "It doesn't catch more of it. The flap could aim extra sunlight down into the box."

This time, Amara studied the drawing. "Show me," she said.

They propped the flap open with a ruler and angled it toward the sun. Joon adjusted it every few minutes as the light shifted. Before the bell rang, their second chocolate square had melted into a glossy puddle.

They did not win; the other teams had finished first. But on the walk back inside, Amara asked Joon what else was in his notebook.$q$, 'draft')
