-- Passage rg6-word-parts-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('98c91e10-4d4f-529e-8c24-b36cde61f489', 'rg6-word-parts-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-word-parts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Wheels Through Time$q$, $q$The first bicycles appeared in Europe in the early 1800s. They had no pedals at all. Riders sat on a wooden frame and pushed against the ground with their feet, a bit like a scooter.

Later inventors added pedals to the front wheel. To go faster, some makers built a giant front wheel and a tiny rear wheel. These tall machines were hard to climb onto and easy to fall from, so many riders found them unsafe.

The safety bicycle changed everything. It had two wheels of equal size and a chain that turned the back wheel. Riders could now sit low and put their feet on the ground when they stopped. Soon, riding became popular with people of all ages.

Bicycles also helped people who could not afford a horse. Workers could ride to jobs that were too far to walk. Some riders formed clubs and took long trips into the countryside.

Not everyone needed two wheels. Young children often started on a tricycle, which stood steady on its own. Some performers learned to ride a unicycle, balancing on a single wheel while they juggled.

Today, bicycles are still one of the most efficient ways to travel. A cyclist uses very little energy compared to a walker covering the same distance. Many cities now build bike lanes to make riding safer.$q$, 'draft')
