-- Passage rg7-argument-evidence-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('01ba47a1-7811-5fb6-8e7f-90c7299862e4', 'rg7-argument-evidence-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Leave the Leaves$q$, $q$Each fall, many families rake every leaf from their yards, stuff the leaves into bags and leave the bags at the curb. Homeowners should stop doing this. Instead, they should leave at least some fallen leaves on the ground through the winter.

Fallen leaves are a home for small animals. Many moths and butterflies spend the winter as eggs or cocoons tucked into leaf piles. Salamanders, beetles and spiders hide there as well. When the leaves are bagged and hauled away, these creatures go with them.

Leaves also feed the soil. As they break down, they release nutrients that trees and other plants use in spring. A layer of leaves also keeps the soil from drying out and protects plant roots from hard frosts.

Bagging leaves creates waste, too. In the town of Pinecrest, the public works department collects about 900 tons of yard waste every fall, and much of it is leaves. Trucks burn fuel driving those bags to a disposal site.

Some people say a yard covered in leaves looks messy. A thick, wet layer on a lawn can also smother the grass. These are fair points, but there is a middle path. Homeowners can rake leaves off the lawn and spread them under trees and in flower beds, where they do the most good.

Leaving the leaves saves time, helps wildlife and feeds the soil. This fall, put down the rake for at least part of the yard.$q$, 'draft')
