-- Passage rg7-argument-evidence-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('768e6136-f3a5-5735-8ec2-1ba473a9d964', 'rg7-argument-evidence-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Can a Game App Sharpen Your Brain?$q$, $q$A company that sells a memory game app recently launched an advertisement with a bold claim. It says that playing its games for ten minutes a day will "make your whole brain sharper." Before you download it, it is worth looking closely at the reasons the company gives.

The advertisement offers three pieces of support. First, it says that players' scores on the app's games rise steadily over a month. Second, it shares a message from a user named Tomasz, who says he has felt more focused since he started playing. Third, it states that "scientists agree the brain can grow and change."

Each of these sounds convincing at first, but each has a problem. Rising scores show that players get better at the app's own games. That is expected with any kind of practice. They do not show that players get better at other tasks, such as remembering a science lesson or a phone number.

Tomasz's message is one person's feeling. He may be telling the truth, but one story cannot show what will happen for most players. He might also feel more focused for another reason, such as getting more sleep.

The last statement is true in general. Brains do change as people learn. However, that fact does not show that this particular app causes useful changes.

To support its claim, the company would need a fair test. Researchers could give one group the app and another group a different activity, then compare how both groups do on everyday memory tasks.

Until then, the app may be fun to play. Just do not expect it to make your whole brain sharper.$q$, 'draft')
