-- Passage rg7-authors-purpose-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ad71e547-42c8-528d-83b3-7fbbb33d66e0', 'rg7-authors-purpose-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Case for Paper Notes$q$, $q$In many classrooms, students now take notes on laptops or tablets. Typing is fast, and digital notes are easy to search and hard to lose in a messy backpack. Many teachers and students believe typing has made handwritten notes old-fashioned. I disagree. For most class lessons, students learn more when they write notes by hand.

The reason is speed, though not in the way people expect. Because most students type faster than they write, they often try to copy every word a teacher says. Their notes become long, but they do little thinking while they type. Writing by hand is slower, so students must choose what matters. They put ideas into their own words, draw arrows between connected ideas, and skip what is less important. That choosing is a form of learning.

Several studies that compared the two methods have found that students who wrote by hand often remembered the main ideas better a week later. The typed notes were longer, but length did not lead to understanding.

Digital notes do have real strengths. A student who misses class can receive a classmate's typed notes in seconds. Students who have difficulty with handwriting may need a keyboard to keep up, and they should always have that choice. I am not arguing that devices should leave the classroom.

My point is narrower. When the goal is understanding a lesson, rather than simply recording it, a pencil gives the brain more work to do. Students who have the choice should try paper for one week and notice what they remember.$q$, 'draft')
