-- Passage rg7-character-setting-plot-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2b14a01a-2aa6-5090-8790-e88dad9feaeb', 'rg7-character-setting-plot-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Cold Snap$q$, $q$Lina Haddad had grown forty bean seedlings for her science fair project, and every one of them lived in the community greenhouse behind the Maple Street library. The greenhouse was a long plastic tunnel with a small electric heater at one end. On most winter days, it stayed warm enough that Lina could work in a T-shirt.

On Saturday morning, the radio warned of a cold snap. The temperature would drop to ten degrees by nightfall. Lina biked to the greenhouse and found Mr. Achterberg, the volunteer manager, kneeling beside the heater with a flashlight.

"It quit an hour ago," he said. "The repair shop can't come until Monday."

Lina looked down the rows. The seedlings near the door were already drooping. Her project was due in nine days, and she needed two more weeks of growth data. She felt like sitting down on the cold floor and giving up.

Instead, she counted what the greenhouse still had: rain barrels full of water, a stack of old blankets in the shed, and a roll of bubble wrap left over from the library's last book delivery. She remembered from her own research that water holds heat longer than air does.

"What if we fill jugs with warm water from the library sink and set them between the trays?" she asked. "Then we cover everything with blankets for the night."

Mr. Achterberg raised his eyebrows. "That could work. Other gardeners have seedlings in here too."

By sunset, six neighbors had come to carry jugs. They wrapped the tunnel's walls in bubble wrap and tucked blankets over every tray. Lina wrote the temperature in her notebook every hour until her mother came to take her home.

On Monday morning, thirty-six of her forty seedlings were standing straight. Lina added a new section to her project board. She titled it "What Happened When the Heater Failed," and it became the part the judges asked about most.$q$, 'draft')
