-- Passage rg7-character-setting-plot-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('72d1dd22-d603-5e84-bca7-50eae45507b3', 'rg7-character-setting-plot-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Hill Course$q$, $q$Mateo Ruiz had run every practice on the school track, a flat red oval where each lap measured exactly four hundred meters. He liked knowing his pace. He liked the clock at the finish line that told him, to the second, how he was doing.

The district meet was held at Cedar Ridge Park. When the bus pulled into the lot, Mateo stared out the window. The course climbed a grassy hill, dropped into a wooded trail, and crossed a creek on a narrow footbridge. Rain the night before had turned the low spots into mud.

"There are no mile markers," he said to his teammate, Ji-woo Han. "How am I supposed to know my pace?"

Ji-woo shrugged. She had grown up on a farm with a steep pasture behind the barn. "You don't run a hill by the clock," she said. "Short steps going up. Lean forward. Let your legs go loose on the way down."

Mateo went out fast, the way he always did on the track. By the top of the first hill, his lungs burned, and four runners passed him on the slope. On the muddy trail, he slipped twice and nearly lost a shoe.

Halfway through, Ji-woo came up beside him. "Short steps," she said, and moved ahead.

Mateo tried it on the second hill. His strides got smaller, and his breathing steadied. He passed two runners near the top. On the way down, he let his legs go loose, and the ground seemed to pull him forward.

He finished nineteenth, far behind his usual place. But on the bus home, he asked Coach Benally if the team could practice at Cedar Ridge Park once a week.

"I need to learn to run without a clock," he said.$q$, 'draft')
