-- Passage rg7-character-setting-plot-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('19686999-1c1f-5aae-935b-97ee3dc76fe7', 'rg7-character-setting-plot-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Snowbound Press$q$, $q$In the winter of 1891, the town of Harlow Junction depended on the railroad for almost everything: coal, mail, flour, and the large rolls of paper that fed the printing press at the Harlow Weekly Star. So when a storm buried the tracks under six feet of snow, the town was cut off.

Fourteen-year-old Ezra Lindqvist had worked at the Star for only two months. His job was to sweep, sort the metal letters, and ink the press. Mrs. Adaeze Okoro, the owner and editor, did everything else.

"The train won't get through for at least a week," she said on Monday, looking at the last thin roll of paper. "We have enough for one page, not four."

Ezra expected her to cancel the paper. Instead, she sat at her desk and began crossing out articles. A story about a horse show went. So did the advertisements for spring hats.

"People don't need hats right now," she said. "They need to know who has coal to spare, which roads are open, and when the doctor is making visits."

She sent Ezra out on snowshoes to gather that news. He knocked on doors he had never knocked on before. The blacksmith told him which families had run out of firewood. The schoolteacher said she would keep the stove burning for anyone who needed a warm place during the day.

By Thursday night, the single page was printed. Ezra carried the copies himself, house to house, because the delivery wagon could not move.

When the train finally arrived nine days later, Mrs. Okoro offered Ezra a new job title: reporter. He said yes before she finished the sentence.$q$, 'draft')
