-- Passage rg7-character-setting-plot-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8e872f83-0f44-5b24-9beb-350db3b6fcd0', 'rg7-character-setting-plot-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Fox and the Dry Riverbed$q$, $q$Long ago, a river ran through the valley of tall grass, and every animal drank from it whenever it pleased. The fox drank at the deep bend, where the water was coolest. The tortoise drank at the shallow edge, because his legs were short. The two had never spoken, for the fox thought the tortoise was too slow to be worth a conversation.

Then came a summer with no rain. Week by week, the river shrank. The deep bend became a puddle, and then it became cracked mud. The fox ran up and down the valley, searching, but every place he remembered was dry.

At last, tired and thirsty, he came upon the tortoise resting under a rock.

"You look well for someone in a drought," the fox said, surprised.

"I have lived in this valley for eighty summers," said the tortoise. "I have seen the river vanish four times. Each time, water hides under the old willow at the north end, where the ground stays damp. You must dig, and you must be patient."

The fox did not like being told to be patient. Still, he followed the tortoise north. The walk took all afternoon, because the tortoise would not hurry. At the willow, the fox dug with his quick paws while the tortoise pointed out exactly where. Before long, cool water seeped into the hole.

They shared it that evening, and many evenings after, until the rains returned and the river filled again.

The fox went back to the deep bend. But now, each morning, he trotted to the shallow edge first to greet the tortoise.

Moral: The slowest neighbor may know the road you need.$q$, 'draft')
