-- Passage rg7-comparing-texts-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('40453977-697b-5fdf-8d0d-ccec13a09f4c', 'rg7-comparing-texts-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-comparing-texts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$One Room, Every Grade$q$, $q$Text 1: The Winter Term (a story)

Hattie Brandt reached the schoolhouse before sunrise, her boots crunching on the frozen prairie. It was her turn to light the stove. Her older brothers had stayed home until the harvest was in, and now they would come only on the coldest days, when the farm needed them least.

By eight o'clock, nineteen students filled the single room. The smallest ones sat up front, swinging their feet. Hattie sat in the back row with the oldest pupils. Miss Lindqvist, who was only seventeen herself, rang the brass bell.

Hattie loved the moment when Miss Lindqvist said, "Hattie, will you help the little ones with their sums?" She knelt beside six-year-old Pim and guided his chalk across his slate. When he finally wrote the right answer, he grinned so widely that Hattie felt warm all the way through. Someday, she decided, she would have a schoolhouse of her own.

Text 2: Schooling on the Frontier (a factual account)

In the late 1800s, many children in rural parts of the United States attended one-room schools. A single teacher taught every student, often from about age five to age fourteen. Teachers were frequently young, and some were only a few years older than their oldest pupils.

Students usually wrote on small slates with chalk, because paper was costly. Older students often helped younger ones with reading and arithmetic, which let the teacher work with several groups at once. A wood stove heated the room, and students took turns bringing firewood and water.

The school year was often arranged around farm work. Many older children, especially boys, attended mainly in winter, after the crops had been harvested.$q$, 'draft')
