-- Passage rg7-comparing-texts-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c8db193c-32a6-5833-881b-39ee03fbe820', 'rg7-comparing-texts-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-comparing-texts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Keepers of the Light$q$, $q$Text 1: The Log Book (a story)

Marisol Reyes was twelve when her father became keeper of the lighthouse on Gull Point. On her first evening, she climbed the winding iron stairs with him at sunset. Together they filled the lamp with oil and lit the wick. The great glass lens caught the flame and threw a beam far across the dark water.

All night, her father climbed the stairs every few hours to trim the wick so the flame stayed bright. In the morning, Marisol polished soot from the lens with a soft cloth until it sparkled. Then her father opened a thick book and wrote: "Wind from the west. Light fog. Two fishing boats passed safely."

Marisol ran her finger down the page. "Does anyone ever read this?" she asked.

"Maybe not," her father said. "But every boat that passed safely is in here. That is enough for me."

Text 2: Lighthouse Keepers (a factual account)

Before electric lights became common, lighthouses depended on keepers. In the 1800s, a keeper lit the lamp at sunset and kept it burning until sunrise. Lamps burned oil, and their wicks had to be trimmed often so they would not smoke or dim. Because of this task, keepers were sometimes nicknamed "wickies."

Keepers also cleaned soot from the large glass lens and the windows. Most kept a daily logbook recording the weather, passing ships and any problems with the light.

Many keepers lived at the lighthouse with their families. Spouses and children often helped with the work. During the 1900s, most lighthouses were changed to run automatically, and the job of keeper slowly disappeared.$q$, 'draft')
