-- Passage rg7-comparing-texts-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('fdcd65c1-d9d9-5311-8087-81ec281ff0a4', 'rg7-comparing-texts-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-comparing-texts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Night of the Landing$q$, $q$Text 1: Staying Up (a story)

On a warm July night in 1969, nine-year-old Priya Nair was allowed to stay up past midnight for the first time. Her family had borrowed folding chairs from the Okonkwos next door, and now both families crowded around one small television.

The picture was gray and fuzzy. Priya squinted at a ghostly shape moving down a ladder. Then a boot touched the ground, and the whole room went silent. Grandpa Nair, who rarely showed his feelings, wiped his eyes with his sleeve.

Mr. Okonkwo leaned toward Priya. "Remember this," he whispered. "You are watching something no one has ever seen before."

Priya stepped onto the porch afterward and looked up. The Moon looked exactly the same as always. Yet somehow, she thought, it was a different Moon now.

Text 2: Watching Apollo 11 (a factual account)

On July 20, 1969, the Apollo 11 mission landed two astronauts on the Moon. A third astronaut stayed in orbit around the Moon. Hours after landing, the first astronaut climbed down a ladder and stepped onto the surface.

The moonwalk was broadcast live on television. The camera sent black and white images that were often blurry. Its signal traveled about 240,000 miles of space and had to be converted before home sets could show it. In much of the United States, the moonwalk took place late at night.

An estimated 600 million people around the world watched the broadcast. In many places, families and neighbors gathered together around a single set. For many viewers, it became one of the most memorable events of their lives.$q$, 'draft')
